# tools/l/log_has_a_row_witness.rish -- every log has a way in, wherever the law puts it.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. The session-logs law says a log gets a row in the index that promises to hold it,
# so the room and its index describe one set. On `20260824` the 192-byte row bound refused a row
# three times, correctly, and **twice the commit shipped anyway** -- the row-writing step and the
# commit were separate commands, so a refusal in the first did not stop the second. A log with no
# row is a record nobody reaches from the front door, and nothing in this tree could see one.
#
# WHY IT WAS REPOINTED, `20260905`. This rung watched `session-logs/*.kyri` -- the FLAT room -- and
# its control proved a folded log free "because its row is on the day's shelf." That exemption was
# correct and its destination was never checked. Then `20260827.171500` seated BORN ON ITS DAY'S
# SHELF: a log is written straight to `date/YYYYMMDD/`, so the flat room is empty by design. The
# exemption grew to cover every log in the tree, the scan measured `flat_logs=0`, and it reported
# `verdict=ok` for nine days while four logs stood unreachable from any front door --
# `20260828.114450`, `20260829.095622`, `20260903.234946`, `20260904.095307`.
#
# This is the shape REDS %381 found in this rung's own sibling: `index_row_bound` read the pin
# alone, measured `rows=0`, and passed for four days while the open shelf carried 87 rows for 64
# logs. One law moved both guards' subject; one guard was repointed and one was not.
#
# AN EMPTY POPULATION IS THE TELL, and it is the same lesson %416 booked yesterday one room over: a
# gate held at zero cannot tell a clean collection from a dead instrument, because both answer zero,
# and zero is the answer everyone wants to hear. So this rung floors its own reach -- a tree with no
# logs at all refuses, and a scan whose awk died without printing refuses rather than letting every
# empty reading be read as a zero. The second refusal fired on its first run, on an unexpanded glob.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal. Every log under `session-logs/date/YYYYMMDD/` stamped on or after the
# born-on-shelf law is named by a link in an index; every flat log is named by a link in the pin;
# and the living pin describes the shelves it points at -- every day shelf carries a pin row, the
# newest shelf is the day marked open, and a closed day's count equals its shelf's rows. 4,056 logs
# across 44 shelves in 0.17s, one find and one awk rather than a grep per log.
#
# WHY NO CLOSED-SHELF EXEMPTION, where the sibling has one. `index_row_bound` passes a closed shelf
# free because its faults -- a row over its bound, a duplicate stamp -- are repaired only by
# rewriting bytes a closed shelf has promised to keep. A MISSING row is repaired by adding one, and
# accretion is what accrete-never-break asks for rather than what it forbids.
#
# WHAT IS RATCHETED, never gated. Logs on days BEFORE the law, whose shelves were written by bulk
# folds that moved files without carrying every row. **216** stand at seating, 211 of them in the
# `20260712`-`20260717` block, which is reached in PART: no DAY-named index covers it, while the
# aggregate shelf `README-index-through-20260721.md` carries 162 of its 373 logs. A falling ceiling.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. That a row says anything useful about its log. Presence is the check, and the
# 192-byte row bound is what keeps the presence from becoming a paragraph.
#
# Style: context/GAUGE_STYLE.md - Rule: .claude/rules/session-logs.md
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/l/log_has_a_row_witness.rish

say "log-has-a-row: Language EN -- a record nobody can reach from the front door is a record nobody has."
say "log-has-a-row: Style Gauge -- the room, the shelf, and the pin describe one set, and this counts all three."
say "log-has-a-row: Lens TAME -- an empty population is the tell, so the scan floors its own reach."

let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/l/log_has_a_row_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "log-has-a-row: a log stands with no row in the index that promises to hold it, or the pin has drifted from the shelves it describes"
say scan.out
assert scan.out contains "post_law_logs_without_a_row=0" else "log-has-a-row: every log born on a shelf must carry a row on it"
assert scan.out contains "flat_logs_without_a_row=0" else "log-has-a-row: every flat log must be named by the pin"
assert scan.out contains "pin_shelves_missing=0" else "log-has-a-row: every day shelf must carry a row in the living pin"
assert scan.out contains "pin_count_drift=0" else "log-has-a-row: a closed day's pin count must equal its shelf's rows"
assert scan.out contains "verdict=ok" else "log-has-a-row: the scan must balance before it is believed"
say "field GREEN -- every log has a way in, and the pin describes the shelves it points at"

let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/l/log_has_a_row_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "log-has-a-row: the control must complete"
say control.out
assert control.out contains "row_present_free=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a flat log with its row must pass free"
assert control.out contains "log_counted=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a flat log must be counted"
assert control.out contains "no_row_bitten=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a flat log with no row must red"
assert control.out contains "no_row_counted=yes" else "log-has-a-row: the count must name how many logs stand unreachable"
assert control.out contains "no_row_named=yes" else "log-has-a-row: an unreachable log must be named, so its row can be written"
assert control.out contains "one_of_three_found=yes" else "log-has-a-row: one missing among three must be found, never carried by a majority"
assert control.out contains "bron_counted=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a .bron log counts beside a .kyri one"
assert control.out contains "folded_free=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a folded log needs no row in the flat pin"
assert control.out contains "folded_uncounted=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a folded log must not be counted against the flat room"
assert control.out contains "substring_refused=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a substring must never answer for a filename"
assert control.out contains "absent_pin_refused=yes" else "log-has-a-row: an absent pin must refuse rather than read zero logs and call it clean"
say "twelve elder behaviors hold, one of them inverted by the law that moved"

assert control.out contains "shelved_row_free=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a shelved log with its row must pass free"
assert control.out contains "shelved_counted=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a shelved log must be counted"
assert control.out contains "shelf_gap_bitten=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a post-law log with no row must red"
assert control.out contains "shelf_gap_counted=yes" else "log-has-a-row: the post-law gap must be counted"
assert control.out contains "shelf_gap_named=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a post-law gap must be named by its day and file"
assert control.out contains "shelf_one_of_three=yes" else "log-has-a-row: one gap among three on a shelf must be found"
assert control.out contains "elder_counted=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a pre-law gap must be counted as elder"
assert control.out contains "elder_not_post=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a pre-law gap must never be charged to the post-law gate"
assert control.out contains "elder_free_under_ceiling=yes" else "log-has-a-row: elder gaps under the ceiling must pass free"
assert control.out contains "elder_ceiling_free=yes" else "log-has-a-row: the elder ceiling must be free at its own number"
assert control.out contains "elder_ceiling_bitten=yes" else "log-has-a-row: the elder ceiling must bite one past it, so no override exists"
assert control.out contains "empty_room_refused=yes" else "log-has-a-row: an empty room must refuse, where the elder control let it pass"
assert control.out contains "empty_tree_refused=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a tree with no logs must refuse rather than read as spotless"
assert control.out contains "flat_only_tree_free=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a tree keeping its logs flat must pass free -- one floor, not two"
assert control.out contains "no_logs_refused=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a shelf holding no logs must refuse"
assert control.out contains "pin_gap_bitten=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a day shelf the pin never names must red"
assert control.out contains "pin_gap_named=yes" else "log-has-a-row: an unnamed shelf must be named, so its pin row can be written"
assert control.out contains "pin_drift_bitten=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a closed day whose count disagrees with its shelf must red"
assert control.out contains "pin_drift_named=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a drifted count must print both numbers"
assert control.out contains "pin_drift_repaired=yes" else "log-has-a-row: the repair must free the drift, so the gate is not a wall"
assert control.out contains "open_day_count_exempt=yes" else "log-has-a-row: the open day gains a row every lap and must not be count-checked"
assert control.out contains "pin_open_stale_bitten=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a pin marking yesterday open while today stands must red"
assert control.out contains "pin_open_repaired=yes" else "log-has-a-row: naming today open must free the reading"
assert control.out contains "gathering_read_free=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a through- gathering must be read for its links"
assert control.out contains "gathering_links_counted=yes" else "log-has-a-row: a gathering's links must answer for the logs they name"
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "log-has-a-row: the control must agree with itself"
say "thirty-six behaviors proven on planted rooms, shelves and pins, both sides of every refusal"

say "GREEN: log-has-a-row -- every log has a way in, and the pin describes the shelves."
